---

### `ErrorBoundary.tsx`

**How it works:**

| Part | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `getDerivedStateFromError` | Captures the thrown `Error` into state, triggering a re-render into fallback mode |
| `componentDidCatch` | Receives the full `ErrorInfo` (component stack) and logs everything to the console |
| `retry()` | Resets both `error` and `errorInfo` to `null`, which unmounts the fallback and re-renders `children` from scratch |
| `fallback` prop | Optional render-prop escape hatch — receives `{ error, errorInfo, retry }` so you can supply a custom UI |
| `DefaultFallback` | Built-in UI: human-readable message, collapsible component stack, and the retry button |

**Usage:**

```tsx
// Wrap any subtree — one boundary can protect many children
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback via render prop
<ErrorBoundary
  fallback={({ error, retry }) => (
    <div>
      <p>Oops: {error.message}</p>
      <button onClick={retry}>Retry</button>
    </div>
  )}
>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

**Key caveats (React error boundary limitations):**
- Only catches errors during **render, lifecycle methods, and constructors** — not event handlers or async code (use `try/catch` there).
- A class component is required; hooks cannot implement `getDerivedStateFromError` / `componentDidCatch`.
- After a retry, if the child still throws, the boundary catches it again immediately.
